## 📊 Booking Flow: From Seat Selection to Confirmation

Here is how a booking request moves through the system end-to-end — including both failure paths that keep inventory clean:

`````mermaid
flowchart TD
    A([User Selects Seats]) --> B{All seats AVAILABLE?}
    B -- No --> C([Error: Seat Already Held by Another User])
    B -- Yes --> D[Lock Seats  10 min TTL applied]
    D --> E([User Redirected to Payment Page])
    E --> F{Payment Successful?}
    F -- Timeout or Failure --> G[Release Lock  Seats set back to AVAILABLE]
    F -- Yes --> H[Set Seats to BOOKED]
    H --> I([Confirmation Email Sent to User])
    G --> J([Error: Payment Failed  Seats Released])
```

The flowchart maps the complete booking transaction as a directed decision graph with two distinct failure exits. The first diamond — "All seats AVAILABLE?" — is the cheap early-exit: if any seat is already LOCKED or BOOKED by a concurrent user, the request is rejected before a lock is ever acquired on the current user's behalf, requiring zero cleanup. Only after seats are successfully locked does control reach the payment gateway, whose failure branch explicitly releases the hold and returns inventory to AVAILABLE — this explicit release is what separates a well-designed system from one that silently leaks inventory after a card decline.

Two distinct failure paths keep inventory accurate:

1. **Pre-lock failure** — seats are already LOCKED by a concurrent user. The second user receives a graceful "unavailable" response immediately, before any lock is created on their behalf.
2. **Post-lock failure** — the user's payment times out or fails. The LOCKED seats revert to AVAILABLE either via TTL expiry (handled by a background cleanup job) or an explicit release call in the payment failure handler.

The 10-minute lock TTL is the safety net for abandoned sessions: a user who closes the browser mid-checkout will not freeze a seat for the rest of the evening.

